{
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "check": "Exactly one new file exists under upcoming-release-notes/ on this branch, named fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md — a short descriptive slug, not a bare number — and no other note is added, modified, renamed or deleted. Carried forward verbatim from the original criteria; PROVEN BY CI, which QA must cite as \"ci\".",
      "how_to_verify": "CI: the 'Unit tests' step of .github/workflows/ci-verify.yml. For the name-only part: `git diff --name-status origin/main...HEAD -- upcoming-release-notes/` must show exactly one row, `A<TAB>upcoming-release-notes/fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md`.",
      "id": "AC-1",
      "source": "IAC-1",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "check": "The note's front matter is exactly `category: Bugfix` on line 2 and `authors: [Shivam-Fl]` on line 3, and the author is a person (the repository owner, not a bot or agent login). Carried forward verbatim.",
      "how_to_verify": "CI: the 'Unit tests' step. Directly: `head -3 upcoming-release-notes/fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md`. Independently: `BASE_REF=main node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs` prints `Validated 1 release note(s). 🎉` and exits 0; with `authors: [github-actions]` it exits 1 naming the login.",
      "id": "AC-2",
      "source": "IAC-2",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "check": "The note's body is exactly one non-empty line that says what a user sees change and contains no implementation vocabulary. Carried forward verbatim, including its note on the issue's own contradictory wording.",
      "how_to_verify": "CI: the 'Unit tests' step. Directly: `sed -n '6p'` prints the single body line, `sed -n '7p'` nothing, `wc -l` reports 6, and a grep for implementation vocabulary returns 0.",
      "id": "AC-3",
      "source": "IAC-3",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "check": "The repository's release-notes check accepts the note, and it is a check that runs. Carried forward verbatim and NOT rewritten: it is true as written, because ci-verify.yml restores no lage cache, so the gate runs on every CI run. AC-6 is added beside it for the local `yarn test` case it did not cover.",
      "how_to_verify": "CI: the 'Unit tests' step. Second route: `BASE_REF=main node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.mjs` → `Validated 1 release note(s). 🎉`, exit 0; with a two-line body, exit 1 naming the file.",
      "id": "AC-4",
      "source": "IAC-4",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "check": "A release note placed in a subdirectory of upcoming-release-notes/ is held to exactly the same rules as one at the top level. A note at upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested/bad-note.md with `category: Chore` and `authors: [github-actions]` makes `yarn test` exit non-zero and names the file as `qa-nested/bad-note.md`, with forward slashes — the same outcome as the identical bytes at the top level. Nothing may treat a subdirectory as out of scope: the release-notes checker selects such a note through its diff, the changelog generator lists it with `git ls-tree -r`, and count-points.mjs counts it under `upcoming-release-notes/**/*`, so a note the gate cannot see is a note that is published and credited to nobody.",
      "how_to_verify": "(1) `node packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs; echo EXIT=$?` → `Validated 92 release note(s).`, EXIT=0. (2) `mkdir -p upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested` and write bad-note.md with category Chore / author github-actions. (3) Re-run the same command → EXIT=1 with output containing `qa-nested/bad-note.md category` and `qa-nested/bad-note.md authors are not people: github-actions`. (4) `yarn workspace @actual-app/ci-actions run release-notes` → non-zero. (5) `rm -rf upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested` and confirm green again. On PR #91 as shipped, step 3 printed `Tests 8 passed`, GATE_EXIT=0, on these exact bytes.",
      "id": "AC-5",
      "source": "IAC-1",
      "verify": "test"
    },
    {
      "check": "`yarn test` fails on a release note the checker rejects even when nothing inside any workspace package has changed and the lage cache is already warm. Run `lage test --scope @actual-app/ci-actions --continue` once with the note valid and let it cache green; change ONLY the note's body so it spans two physical lines, changing no file under packages/; run the same command again. It must exit non-zero and print `body should contain exactly one line` naming fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md. It must NOT print `» skip @actual-app/ci-actions test`, and it must not exit 0. The same holds through `yarn test` itself. A gate that only fires when its cache is cold is not a gate: this is the change a contributor makes when they add a release note, and it is the change `yarn test` is run to check.",
      "how_to_verify": "With node_modules installed and nothing else touching the tree: `rm -rf node_modules/.cache/lage`; (1) cold run with the valid note → EXIT1=0; (2) append a second body line and confirm `git status --porcelain` lists only that one file; (3) re-run the same lage command → non-zero, output containing `body should contain exactly one line` and the file name, and NOT containing `» skip @actual-app/ci-actions test`; (4) restore the note; (5) `yarn test` → non-zero with the same message. If step 3 prints `» skip`, this criterion fails. On PR #91 as shipped, steps 1-3 gave the same key d1677e96… both times, `» skip` in run 2, and EXIT2=0 on bytes direct vitest rejects.",
      "id": "AC-6",
      "source": "IAC-4",
      "verify": "test"
    }
  ],
  "approach": "Extract the gate into packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs — recursive walk, inclusion rule matching selectReleaseNotePaths (every *.md at any depth, README.md excluded only at the top level), rules from ../src/release-notes/util.mjs plus the two checks validateFile contributes, failure strings keyed by the relative path with forward slashes and otherwise byte-identical to the ones QA's T-8/T-9/T-10 assert, YAML parse errors reported rather than thrown. It runs as a CLI when invoked directly. The vitest block in release-notes-check.test.mjs imports it instead of carrying its own copy, keeps its existing cases, and gains two: one reproducing T-13 by writing upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested/bad-note.md and asserting the failure names `qa-nested/bad-note.md`, and one reproducing T-12's cause structurally by asserting the wiring. Make the gate reachable on a warm cache: add `\"release-notes\": \"node bin/release-notes-gate.mjs\"` to packages/ci-actions/package.json, and a `cache: false` `release-notes` pipeline task that `test` dependsOn, in lage.config.js — verified against lage 2.15.17's source rather than assumed: expandDepSpecs maps a bare dependsOn entry to the same package's task; TargetFactory turns `cache: false` into target.cache = false; SimpleScheduler.getReadyTargets requires every dependency to be successful (WrappedTarget.successful counts skipped) while the cache lookup lives in WrappedTarget.run() after that point, so an uncached dependency runs even when its dependent is a cache hit; a gate failure leaves dependents pending, making runAction set process.exitCode = 1. Packages without the script are skipped cleanly by NpmScriptRunner.shouldRun. The note file itself does not change.",
  "files": [
    {
      "action": "create",
      "change": "New ESM module exporting listNotePaths() (recursive readdirSync, every *.md at any depth, README.md excluded only at the top level — selectReleaseNotePaths' rule — paths relative to the notes dir joined with '/', sorted), validateNote(relPath) (the checker's category/authors/one-line-body rules via ../src/release-notes/util.mjs, failures keyed `${relPath} …` with the exact strings QA's T-8/T-9/T-10 assert, front-matter parse errors reported as `<path> front matter is not valid YAML: <reason>`), collectFailures(), and a main() that prints `::error::<failure>` per failure and exits 1, or prints `Validated N release note(s).` and exits 0, guarded by an import.meta.url check so importing it has no side effects.",
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-gate.mjs",
      "why": "T-13, and the duplication that caused it: one walk and one rule set with two callers, so the gate and the checker cannot drift apart again the way parseReleaseNotes and selectReleaseNotePaths already have. The CLI entry exists for T-12 — the gate must be runnable as its own task, outside the cached test task."
    },
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Leave the existing subprocess-driven describe block untouched. In the describe('the real upcoming-release-notes/ directory') block this PR added, delete the inlined non-recursive readNotes() and validate(name) and import { collectFailures, listNotePaths } from './release-notes-gate.mjs' instead; keep 'contains release notes to check' and keep 'every note carries a category the published changelog has a header for' asserting expect(collectFailures()).toEqual([]). Add it('holds a note in a subdirectory to the same rules') — write upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested/bad-note.md with category Chore and author github-actions, assert collectFailures() includes `qa-nested/bad-note.md`, remove the directory in a finally. Add it('is reachable on every run, not only on a cold cache') — createRequire to read ../../../lage.config.js and this package's package.json, assert config.pipeline['release-notes'].cache === false, that pipeline.test.dependsOn includes 'release-notes', and that pkg.scripts['release-notes'] mentions release-notes-gate.mjs. Comment both with the defect each reproduces; run npx oxfmt and commit the formatting.",
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs",
      "why": "T-13's reproduction and T-12's cause, restated as cases that go red if either defect returns — neither can be expressed by the existing block, since the nested case needs a note that is not there and the cache defect is a property of the task graph, not of any note."
    },
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Add one key to scripts, beside test and typecheck: \"release-notes\": \"node bin/release-notes-gate.mjs\". Nothing else changes; test stays vitest --run and no dependency changes.",
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/package.json",
      "why": "lage runs an npmScript task only for packages that define it — NpmScriptRunner.shouldRun returns false when package.json.scripts[task] is absent — so this single key scopes the new task to this package and leaves every other workspace untouched."
    },
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Add a 'release-notes' pipeline task with `type: 'npmScript'` and `cache: false`, commented with the reason (it reads upcoming-release-notes/, which lives at the repo root outside every workspace package, so no note can appear in a test task's hash and a cache hit would skip the gate on the one change it exists to police — the same reasoning as the existing build:browser entry), and give `test` a `dependsOn: ['release-notes']` alongside its existing options.outputGlob. Change nothing else.",
      "path": "lage.config.js",
      "why": "T-12. The gate cannot be fixed by moving it and cannot be given inputs that reach it (root-level files bucket under a non-target key). Root package.json is refused by reservedChanges(), which flags pre/post hooks of any gating script, and .github/** is forbidden to every plan, so this file is the only place the fix can live."
    },
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "No change. Leave byte-identical to what the PR already added: front matter category: Bugfix / authors: [Shivam-Fl], one blank line, the single body line, one trailing newline. Confirm with git diff --name-status origin/main...HEAD -- upcoming-release-notes/ that the branch still shows exactly one A row.",
      "path": "upcoming-release-notes/fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md",
      "why": "AC-1..AC-3 assert these exact bytes off the branch. Both bugs are in the gate, not the note, and touching it would risk AC-1's 'exactly one new file' for nothing."
    }
  ],
  "issue": 89,
  "root_cause": "Two independent defects, both introduced by the gate this PR added to packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs. (1) NON-RECURSIVE ENUMERATION — readNotes() enumerates upcoming-release-notes/ one level deep, and its inclusion rule was copied from parseReleaseNotes (also non-recursive) rather than from selectReleaseNotePaths (util.mjs:148-149, fed by a recursive `git diff` pathspec, excluding only the top-level README.md); the two functions that read the same directory already disagree and the gate copied the wrong one, so a nested note is checked by the release-notes checker, counted by count-points.mjs and published in the changelog while escaping the gate. (2) THE GATE LIVES INSIDE A CACHED TASK WHOSE INPUTS CANNOT CONTAIN A RELEASE NOTE — `yarn test` is `lage test --continue`; lage hashes each target's inputs, and by construction those inputs can never include upcoming-release-notes/**, because PackageTree.addToPackageTree buckets every tracked file under the nearest ancestor workspace package and the notes directory has none. A note-only change leaves the hash untouched, lage prints `» skip @actual-app/ci-actions test - <same hash>` and exits 0 on a note the checker rejects — the gate is a no-op on precisely the change it exists to police.",
  "tests": [
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "reproduces T-13 — 'holds a note in a subdirectory to the same rules': writes upcoming-release-notes/qa-nested/bad-note.md with category Chore and author github-actions, asserts collectFailures() reports it as `qa-nested/bad-note.md` with forward slashes, removes the directory in a finally. Fails against the gate as shipped, where a non-recursive readdirSync sees 0 nested notes (gap-nested-note.txt: Tests 8 passed, GATE_EXIT=0, on these bytes)",
        "reproduces T-12's cause — 'is reachable on every run, not only on a cold cache': asserts lage.config.js declares a `release-notes` task with cache: false, that pipeline.test.dependsOn includes it, and that this package's package.json defines the script. Fails against PR #91, where the gate exists only inside the cached test task and lage prints `» skip @actual-app/ci-actions test - <same hash>` and exits 0 on a note-only change (hostile-cache-cold-isolation.txt: EXIT2=0 against bytes direct vitest rejects with exit 1)",
        "kept — 'contains release notes to check': listNotePaths() is non-empty so a moved or mis-resolved NOTES_DIR cannot let the rule case pass vacuously",
        "kept — 'every note carries a category the published changelog has a header for': collectFailures() is empty over the real directory, with per-note failure strings naming the offending file rather than throwing on the first",
        "kept unchanged — the six existing subprocess-driven cases against throwaway git repos, which prove the checker's own diff selection and rule wording",
        "confirmed by running against the shipped gate — a two-line body fails with `fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md body should contain exactly one line` (neg-control-A-two-line-body.txt)",
        "confirmed by running against the shipped gate — a github-actions author fails with `fix-custom-report-show-empty-rows-categories.md authors are not people: github-actions` (neg-control-B-bot-author.txt)"
      ],
      "path": "packages/ci-actions/bin/release-notes-check.test.mjs"
    }
  ],
  "understanding": "The previous plan's diagnosis survives: PR #9 shipped a user-visible custom-report fix with nothing under upcoming-release-notes/, and nothing this repository runs fails on a missing note. Its approach shipped a gate with two holes, both of which QA measured. (1) The gate enumerated upcoming-release-notes/ with a single non-recursive fs.readdirSync, so a note in a subdirectory is validated by the release-notes checker, counted by count-points.mjs and published in the changelog while escaping the unit gate (T-13). (2) The gate lives inside the `test` pipeline task, which lage caches — and no release note can ever enter that task's hash: lage 2.15.17's PackageTree.addToPackageTree buckets each `git ls-files` entry under its nearest ancestor workspace package, and the repo-root notes directory has none, so every note buckets under \"\", which is never a target. A note-only change therefore leaves the hash untouched and `yarn test` exits 0 on a note the checker rejects (T-12, reproduced three times by QA; confirmed by me from lage's shipped source). This makes the previous plan's central claim — that the suite \"runs in CI on every PR from here on\" — true only on a cold cache. T-12's literal assertion failure is its control, not the behaviour under test: `withNote` is async with a synchronous body, so the note is already restored when the control's vitestGate() runs. That is a QA spec bug, and it does not clear the product defect, since QA's three hostile evidence files reproduce it without the ordering mistake. Neither failure is a wrong acceptance criterion, so none is rewritten: AC-1..AC-4 are kept verbatim and AC-5 (nested-note escape) and AC-6 (cache-skipped gate) are added after them. Limits: this checkout has no node_modules, so the nested-note blindness was reproduced directly with a standalone node script while the cache behaviour was read out of the exact lage version the lockfile pins rather than executed; AC-6 is written as the command sequence that will actually execute it.",
  "version": 2,
  "mode": "committed"
}
